          </div>
          <QuoteForm city="Los Osos" title="Request a free estimate" variant="hero" />
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="handle-title">
      <div class="container container--prose">
        <h2 id="handle-title" class="section-title">Electrical work I handle in Los Osos</h2>
        <p class="lead-copy">I'm Adam Keif, a C-10 licensed residential electrician based in San Luis Obispo. Los Osos is about fifteen minutes down Los Osos Valley Road from my base, so I am over there regularly. The work here is mostly in existing homes. A large part of Los Osos was effectively under a building moratorium for more than 35 years, from 1988 until the community plan was approved in 2024, so very little new housing went up in that time. That shapes what I get called for.</p>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li><strong>Troubleshooting.</strong> Outlets that stopped working on the side of the house that faces the bay, breakers that trip when the heater comes on, lights that flicker in one room. I find the cause instead of patching the symptom. <a href="/electrical-repair-san-luis-obispo" class="inline-link">Troubleshooting &amp; repair</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Panel upgrades and replacements.</strong> Older Los Osos homes can still have the original 100-amp service, sized for a very different household load. If you are adding an EV charger or a heat pump, or an insurer or inspector has raised the panel, I handle the upgrade, the county permit, and the inspection. <a href="/electrical-panel-upgrade-san-luis-obispo" class="inline-link">Panel upgrades</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>EV charger installation.</strong> Level 2 charging at home, sized to what your panel can actually support. On a small Baywood lot that often means a charger on the driveway side of the house rather than in a garage. <a href="/ev-charger-installation-san-luis-obispo" class="inline-link">EV charger installation</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Lighting.</strong> Recessed lighting in low ceilings, under-cabinet lighting, and exterior fixtures and hardware selected to hold up better in a coastal, salt-air environment. <a href="/lighting-installation-san-luis-obispo" class="inline-link">Recessed &amp; custom lighting</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Exterior and corrosion repairs.</strong> Rusted meter mains, weathered outdoor outlets that no longer hold a GFCI reset, fixture boxes that have turned to powder. Coastal problems, fixed with the right materials.</li>
          <li><strong>Backup power.</strong> Generator interlocks and transfer switches so you can keep the essentials running during a PG&amp;E outage without backfeeding the line.</li>
          <li><strong>Remodels and additions.</strong> New circuits for a kitchen or bath remodel, a converted garage, or a studio out back. With new residential development now moving through the County’s growth-allocation process, full new-home wiring too. <a href="/new-construction-electrical-san-luis-obispo" class="inline-link">New construction &amp; remodels</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section content-section--alt" aria-labelledby="homes-title">
      <div class="container container--prose">
        <h2 id="homes-title" class="section-title">A town of 1960s to 1990s homes, wired for a different era</h2>
        <p>Los Osos grew rapidly through the 1970s and 1980s, then development largely stopped after the 1988 building moratorium. The sewer came online in 2016, and the moratorium was lifted in 2024 with the approval of the community plan, though new development is still subject to County growth allocations, water requirements, and habitat mitigation. The result is a housing stock weighted toward homes built between the 1960s and the 1990s, many of them still on their original panel.</p>
        <p>The neighborhoods differ in the details. Baywood Park, down near the bay and Second Street, has many of the older and smaller houses, on small lots where exterior electrical equipment sits close to the water. Up the hill in Cabrillo Estates the homes are generally larger and on bigger parcels. In between, along Los Osos Valley Road and the streets off South Bay Boulevard toward Monta&ntilde;a de Oro, there is a lot of mid-century housing that has been remodeled over the years without the electrical service ever being upgraded.</p>
        <p>Two things follow from that. First, a lot of the work here is upgrade work: bringing an older panel and its circuits up to what a household actually runs today. Second, the marine environment is hard on outdoor electrical equipment. Meter equipment, exterior receptacles, and light fixtures can corrode faster here than they would inland. Both are routine for me. If you are not sure what shape your house is in, that is a normal place to start, and I can give you a straight answer.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="coastal-title">
      <div class="container container--prose">
        <h2 id="coastal-title" class="section-title">Salt air, older panels, and aluminum wiring</h2>
        <p>Three things come up on the coast that inland jobs rarely involve.</p>
        <h3 class="sub-title">Exterior panels and meter mains in salt air</h3>
        <p>Exterior electrical equipment near the bay takes a beating. Surface rust on a panel door is different from corrosion inside the enclosure, at the bus bars, the breaker terminals, or the neutral lugs, and the second kind is usually hidden until someone opens the panel. When I replace a coastal panel I use a rated enclosure, seal the penetrations, and set it so water has somewhere to go besides inside. The same thinking applies to outdoor receptacles and fixtures: the right box, the right cover, the right gasket.</p>
        <h3 class="sub-title">Aluminum branch wiring</h3>
        <p>Some homes built or rewired during the late 1960s and early 1970s used aluminum branch-circuit wiring for ordinary 15 and 20 amp circuits, and Los Osos added a lot of housing in those years. Aluminum expands, contracts, and oxidizes at connections, which is why the classic symptom is a warm outlet or a switch that works intermittently. It does not automatically mean a full rewire. Depending on the condition of the wiring, I can identify the problem and recommend an approved repair method or, where it is warranted, rewiring.</p>
        <h3 class="sub-title">Backup power for outages</h3>
        <p>People here ask about generators. For many homes the practical answer is a portable generator with a properly installed interlock or manual transfer switch, so you can safely run essential loads such as a refrigerator, a pump, lights, and selected outlets without backfeeding the utility lines. That is a modest, permitted job. A whole-house standby unit is a bigger project, and I will tell you if it is more than you need.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section content-section--alt" aria-labelledby="common-title">
      <div class="container container--prose">
        <h2 id="common-title" class="section-title">Electrical problems common in older Los Osos homes</h2>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li><strong>Breakers that trip repeatedly.</strong> Usually a circuit carrying more than it was designed for, or a fault worth finding rather than resetting.</li>
          <li><strong>Aging 100-amp service.</strong> Enough for the household it was sized for, tight for one running a heat pump, an induction range, or a car charger.</li>
          <li><strong>Aluminum branch wiring.</strong> Warm outlets or switches that work intermittently, in homes wired in the late 1960s and early 1970s.</li>
          <li><strong>Corrosion at exterior equipment.</strong> Receptacles that will not hold a GFCI reset, rusted fixture boxes, corrosion inside a meter enclosure.</li>
          <li><strong>Not enough circuits.</strong> Kitchens, garages, and home offices running off circuits that predate the equipment plugged into them.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="howiwork-title">
      <div class="container container--prose">
        <h2 id="howiwork-title" class="section-title">How I work</h2>
        <p>You call or send a quote request. For simple repairs I can often quote by phone. For a panel upgrade, a generator setup, or an aluminum wiring assessment, I will come out and look, at no cost.</p>
        <p>You get a written quote before any work begins. Flat-rate where possible, no add-ons you did not agree to. For troubleshooting, I charge a diagnostic fee that applies toward the repair if you move forward.</p>
        <p>Permit requirements depend on the work. Panel replacements, EV chargers, and many new electrical installations require a permit. Los Osos is unincorporated, so permits are handled through San Luis Obispo County Planning &amp; Building rather than a city building department. I pull the permit and handle the inspection when one is required. You do not have to deal with any of it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="why-section" aria-labelledby="why-title">
      <div class="container container--prose">
        <h2 id="why-title" class="section-title">Why Los Osos homeowners hire Keif Electric</h2>
        <ul class="why-list">
          <li><strong>You hire me, not a crew.</strong> The person who quotes your job is the person who does it.</li>
          <li><strong>Licensed and insured.</strong> California C-10, CA# 1152084.</li>
          <li><strong>Honest about scope.</strong> If you don't need a full upgrade, I'll tell you. If you do, I'll show you why.</li>
          <li><strong>Clean, labeled work.</strong> Panels left neat and every circuit labeled, so the next person who opens it knows what's what.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="faq-inner">
          <aside class="faq-sidebar">
            <h2 id="faq-title" class="faq-sidebar__title">Los Osos FAQ</h2>
            <p>Have something else? Call and ask.</p>
            <a href="tel:+18052860477" class="btn-link">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.92v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.18 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.63-3.07 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6 19.79 19.79 0 0 1-3.07-8.67A2 2 0 0 1 4.11 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.72c.13.96.37 1.9.72 2.81a2 2 0 0 1-.45 2.11L8.09 9.91a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.27-1.27a2 2 0 0 1 2.11-.45c.91.35 1.85.59 2.81.72A2 2 0 0 1 22 16.92z"/></svg>
              (805) 286-0477
            </a>
          </aside>
          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item" open>
              <summary class="faq-item__btn"><span>Does electrical work in Los Osos require a permit?</span><span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
              <div class="faq-item__body"><p>Permit requirements depend on the work. Panel replacements, EV chargers, and many new electrical installations require one. Los Osos is unincorporated, so permits are handled through San Luis Obispo County Planning &amp; Building rather than a city building department. I handle the permit and inspection process when one is required.</p></div>
            </details>
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary class="faq-item__btn"><span>Why do electrical panels in Los Osos have corrosion problems?</span><span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
              <div class="faq-item__body"><p>Los Osos is a coastal community, and salt air and moisture are hard on exterior electrical equipment. Surface rust on a panel door is different from corrosion inside the enclosure, at the bus bars, or at the connections. I can inspect the panel and tell you which one you have and what needs attention.</p></div>
            </details>
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary class="faq-item__btn"><span>Should I be concerned about aluminum wiring in my Los Osos home?</span><span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
              <div class="faq-item__body"><p>Some homes built or rewired during the late 1960s and early 1970s used aluminum branch-circuit wiring, and Los Osos added a lot of housing in those years. It does not automatically mean you need a full rewire. I can inspect the wiring, tell you what condition it is in, and recommend an approved repair method or replacement where it is warranted.</p></div>
            </details>
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary class="faq-item__btn"><span>Can I install a generator at my Los Osos home?</span><span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
              <div class="faq-item__body"><p>Yes. For many homes a portable generator with a properly installed interlock or manual transfer switch is enough to run essential loads such as a refrigerator, a pump, lights, and selected outlets safely, without backfeeding the utility lines. The right setup depends on what you want to keep running and the electrical service at your home.</p></div>
            </details>
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary class="faq-item__btn"><span>What areas do you serve?</span><span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
              <div class="faq-item__body"><p>Los Osos and Baywood Park, plus <a href="/electrician-morro-bay" class="inline-link">Morro Bay</a>, and from my base in <a href="/electrician-san-luis-obispo" class="inline-link">San Luis Obispo</a> the rest of SLO County. Not sure I reach you? Call and ask.</p></div>
            </details>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="page-cta" id="contact" aria-labelledby="cta-title">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="page-cta__inner">
          <div class="page-cta__copy">
            <h2 id="cta-title" class="page-cta__title"><span class="page-cta__call">Call</span><a class="page-cta__num" href="tel:+18052860477">(805) 286-0477</a></h2>
            <p class="page-cta__lead">I quote the job myself, pull the county permit when one is required, and do the work. Los Osos is about fifteen minutes from my base in San Luis Obispo.</p>
            <a class="page-cta__alt" href="sms:+18052860477">Or text me at the same number.</a>
            <ul class="page-cta__facts">
              <li>California C-10 #1152084</li>
              <li>Liability insured</li>
              <li>Mon&ndash;Fri, 9am&ndash;5pm</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="page-cta__card">
            <div class="page-cta__owner">
            <img class="page-cta__avatar" src="/assets/adam-keif.jpg" alt="" width="112" height="112" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="page-cta__owner-text"><strong>Adam Keif, owner.</strong> Reads every request and calls back the same business day.</p>
          </div>
            <QuoteForm city="Los Osos" title="Or send it and I will call you" variant="hero" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  <noscript>
    <style>
      .content-section .container--prose,
      .why-section .container--prose,
      .proof-placeholder .container,
      .faq-inner,
    </style>
  </noscript>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .container { max-width: var(--max); margin: 0 auto; padding-left: var(--gutter); padding-right: var(--gutter); }
  .container--prose { max-width: 760px; }
  .breadcrumb { background: var(--ke-bone); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-1); padding: 12px 0; }
  .breadcrumb .container { display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: center; font-size: 13px; color: var(--fg-2); }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--ke-orange-text); font-weight: 500; }
  .breadcrumb a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .svc-hero { position: relative; background: var(--ke-bone); overflow: hidden; padding: 72px 0 88px; }
  .svc-hero::before { content: ''; position: absolute; top: -25%; right: -12%; width: 62%; height: 130%; background: radial-gradient(closest-side, rgba(243, 152, 31, 0.12), transparent 72%); pointer-events: none; }
  .svc-hero__inner { position: relative; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 0.9fr; gap: 56px; align-items: center; }
  .svc-hero__title { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-weight: 800; font-size: clamp(38px, 4.4vw, 62px); line-height: 1.04; letter-spacing: -0.025em; color: var(--ke-ink); margin: 0; }
  .svc-hero__line { display: block; }
  .svc-hero__line--accent { color: var(--ke-orange); }
  .svc-hero__dot { display: inline-block; width: 0.16em; height: 0.16em; background: var(--ke-orange); border-radius: 2px; margin-left: 0.05em; vertical-align: baseline; }
  .svc-hero__place { display: block; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-weight: 600; font-size: clamp(15px, 1.6vw, 19px); letter-spacing: 0; color: var(--fg-2); margin: 16px 0 0; }
  .svc-hero__sub { font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ke-ink); max-width: 46ch; margin: 22px 0 26px; font-weight: 500; }
  .svc-hero__ctas { display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .svc-hero__trust { margin-top: 26px; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--fg-2); letter-spacing: 0.01em; }
  .svc-hero__card { justify-self: end; width: 100%; max-width: 460px; background: #fff; border: 1px solid var(--border-1); border-radius: var(--radius-4); box-shadow: var(--shadow-3); padding: 24px 24px 22px; }
  .svc-hero__owner { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; margin-bottom: 18px; padding-bottom: 18px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-1); }
  .svc-hero__avatar { width: 56px; height: 56px; border-radius: 50%; object-fit: cover; object-position: 50% 12%; flex: none; border: 2px solid var(--ke-bone); box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--border-1); }
  .svc-hero__owner-text { margin: 0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--fg-2); }
  .svc-hero__owner-text strong { color: var(--ke-ink); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .svc-hero__line, .svc-hero__place, .svc-hero__sub, .svc-hero__ctas, .svc-hero__trust, .svc-hero__card { opacity: 0; animation: heroIn 0.8s var(--ease-emph) forwards; }
    .svc-hero__line:nth-child(1) { animation-delay: 0.13s; }
    .svc-hero__line:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: 0.21s; }
    .svc-hero__place { animation-delay: 0.29s; }
    .svc-hero__sub { animation-delay: 0.37s; }
    .svc-hero__ctas { animation-delay: 0.45s; }
    .svc-hero__trust { animation-delay: 0.53s; }
    .svc-hero__card { animation-delay: 0.22s; animation-duration: 1s; }
    .content-section .container--prose, .why-section .container--prose, .proof-placeholder .container, .faq-inner { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(22px); transition: opacity 0.7s var(--ease-emph), transform 0.7s var(--ease-emph); }
    .is-in { opacity: 1 !important; transform: none !important; }
  }
  @keyframes heroIn { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(16px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
  .btn { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-weight: 600; font-size: 15px; padding: 13px 18px; border-radius: var(--radius-2); border: 1px solid transparent; cursor: pointer; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 9px; line-height: 1; min-height: 48px; transition: all var(--dur-1) var(--ease-out); text-decoration: none; white-space: nowrap; }
  .btn svg { width: 18px; height: 18px; flex: none; }
  .btn--primary { background: var(--ke-orange); color: #fff; }
  .btn--outline { background: transparent; color: var(--ke-ink); border: 1.5px solid var(--ke-ink); }
  .btn--outline:hover { background: var(--ke-ink); color: var(--ke-bone); }
  .btn--primary:hover { background: var(--ke-orange-dark); }
  .btn--primary:active { transform: translateY(1px); }
  .btn--outline-light { background: transparent; color: var(--ke-bone); border-color: rgba(246, 241, 232, 0.4); }
  .btn--outline-light:hover { background: rgba(246, 241, 232, 0.08); border-color: var(--ke-bone); }
  .btn--lg { padding: 16px 22px; font-size: 16px; border-radius: var(--radius-3); min-height: 54px; }
  .btn-link { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; color: var(--ke-orange); font-weight: 600; font-size: 15px; text-decoration: none; }
